// Lesson 2.3 — Healing Pickups & the Max-Health Cap
// 3-way comparative analysis: Coin vs Spike vs Potion.
// GROUND TRUTH: there is no Potion prefab, no Potion tag, and no potion sprite
// anywhere in the project. The student creates all three by tinting an existing
// sprite (greyboxing) and places instances in the Overworld themselves.
// Health and the max-health cap live on PlayerController; StatManager stays
// empty until the Unit 5 refactor.
//
// Scaffolding: OUTLINE ONLY (level 3). The spec and the API are given and no
// code is. The student built a pickup in 2.1 and a method that changes health in
// 2.2, so this lesson names the file, the method and the decision, and stops
// there. The ask banner is the level marker — a level-3 lesson must not render a
// walkthrough, and verify-site refuses one that does.
//
// The old "Read Function-Level Examples" activity has been deleted (a dam, a
// scooter battery, a spaceship thruster, an overflow alarm). Four foreign
// snippets plus a prediction quiz taught the wrong code shape at exactly the
// moment the student should be writing their own file. The three facts those
// snippets carried now live in the reference table's "what it costs" column and
// one callout info: Mathf.Min bounds one end, Mathf.Clamp bounds both in a
// single call, and an `if` can report a refusal where a method that returns a
// number cannot.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The recurring two-beat block: split the problem, look each part up, connect
// it to something already built. Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each
// part DOES and asks the student to say it — the parts and their doc pointers
// are the level-3 bargain. Every doc pointer here is either a lesson they have
// finished or a row of this lesson's own reference table.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big: 'I want a potion that heals me when I walk into it, and I want my health to stop at the maximum and never go over it — no matter how many potions I find.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Make the potion something the player can touch',
      does: 'A sprite tinted so it cannot be mistaken for a coin, a trigger collider on the root, and a tag of its own — created, prefabbed, and placed in the scene.',
      doc: '2.1 — Coins: Triggers, Tags & Your First Stat (the pickup recipe)',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Recognize the potion in the callback',
      does: '`OnTriggerEnter2D` fires on the first frame the shapes overlap; the `CompareTag` check is what separates a potion from a spike or a wall.',
      doc: 'the `OnTriggerEnter2D` and `CompareTag` rows of this lesson\'s reference table',
    },
    {
      name: 'Change the health value',
      does: 'One field holds health and one method changes it by a signed amount, so the spike and the potion call the same method with different signs.',
      doc: '2.2 — Spikes: Physics, Damage & Death',
      href: '2.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Hold the value under its ceiling',
      does: 'The rule: after any change, health never sits above the maximum. Applied inside the method that changes the value, so no future caller can bypass it.',
      doc: 'the `Mathf.Clamp`, `Mathf.Min`, `Mathf.Max` and selection rows of this lesson\'s reference table',
    },
    {
      name: 'Consume the potion',
      does: 'Destroy the potion\'s GameObject rather than the player\'s, so it cannot be collected twice.',
      doc: 'the `Destroy` row of this lesson\'s reference table',
    },
    {
      name: 'Test it at the boundary',
      does: 'Heal from below the maximum, then heal at the maximum, and confirm the rule holds both times.',
      doc: 'the `Mathf.Clamp` row of this lesson\'s reference table, and the full-health test at the end of the Build Lab',
    },
  ],
  why: 'The split follows the life of one value: an object is created, touched, recognized, changes a number, and dies. Five of those six parts are things you have already built — which is why the lesson is about the one part that is new, the ceiling, rather than about potions.',
  sizeTest: 'The split is finished when each part can be looked up on its own: every part above names either a component, an API call, or a method you have already written. If a part needs the word "and" twice to describe it, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'In one line: what is the exploit that a potion without a ceiling creates?',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>2.3 — Healing Pickups &amp; the Max-Health Cap</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You can gain wealth and lose health. Now you need a way to get health back — and the
    moment you add healing, you create a new kind of problem. Healing without a limit is not
    a feature, it is an exploit. Today you compare the potion against both things you have
    already built, then discover that a value with a ceiling needs a <em>rule</em>, not just
    arithmetic.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I heal, and how do I cap health at a maximum?', 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderDecompose('2.3', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Comparative Thinking</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Good engineers do not design from nothing. When you meet a new mechanic, ask what you
        have already built that behaves like it:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Comparison</th><th>Analysis</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion like the coin?</strong></td>
            <td>Both are <strong>pickups</strong>: trigger colliders, handled by <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code>, and destroyed on contact so they cannot be taken twice. Almost all of your coin code is reusable.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion like the spike?</strong></td>
            <td>Both change <strong>health</strong> — and health lives on <code>PlayerController</code>, exactly where your coin count lives.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion different from both?</strong></td>
            <td>It raises a value that now has a <strong>ceiling</strong>. Without a rule, ten potions means ten times the health, and the game stops being a game.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>There is no potion art in this project — you are going to make one.</strong>
        There is no Potion prefab, no Potion tag and no potion sprite in
        <code>Assets/Artwork</code>. So: pick a sprite you <em>do</em> have, and tint it with the
        Sprite Renderer's <strong>Color</strong> field so it cannot be mistaken for a coin.
        Create the tag yourself. Building a mechanic with borrowed art is called
        <strong>greyboxing</strong>, and it is what professionals do all day — the mechanic is
        the work; the art is a placeholder until someone draws the real thing.
        <br><br>
        The same goes for the world: nothing is a potion until you have built the object
        <em>and</em> dragged instances of it into the Overworld. The spec below ends with that
        step, and skipping it means a mechanic nobody can walk into.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Similar, Different, and Why It Matters</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The potion reuses the coin's trigger, its callback, and its destroy. What is the <em>only</em> thing that genuinely differs?</li>
            <li>Without a ceiling, a player who collects ten potions has ten times the health. Which breaks first — the challenge, the economy, or the feedback the player gets?</li>
            <li>The rule "health must never exceed the maximum" has to live <em>somewhere</em>. Where could it live, and which of those places keeps the rule true no matter which script calls the healing method?</li>
            <li>If a second healing source arrives in Unit 5 — a heart, a shrine, a level-up — is a ceiling check inside the <em>potion</em> script still enough? What does your answer say about where rules belong?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: The API for Bounding a Value</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        This is the level-3 substitute for a walkthrough. The API is laid out below and the
        code is not: you write the file. Three tools can hold a number inside a range, and one
        selection statement is not a tool at all — it is the thing that can do what none of
        the three can.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>Signature</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Mathf.Clamp</code></td>
            <td><code>int Mathf.Clamp(int value, int min, int max)</code> — float overloads exist too</td>
            <td>Returns the value pushed inside the range. The only tool here that holds <strong>both ends in one call</strong>, and the integer overload means health never has to become a decimal.</td>
            <td>It hands back a number and nothing else. It cannot tell you a heal was wasted, and a lower bound of 0 will quietly swallow the negative that an overkill counter might have wanted to see.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Mathf.Min</code></td>
            <td><code>int Mathf.Min(int a, int b)</code></td>
            <td>Returns the smaller of the two — a <strong>ceiling</strong> in a single expression.</td>
            <td>One end only. Nothing in it stops a value running off the bottom, so spikes plus potions means a second tool beside it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Mathf.Max</code></td>
            <td><code>int Mathf.Max(int a, int b)</code></td>
            <td>Returns the larger of the two — a <strong>floor</strong>, and the tool for "never below zero" on its own terms.</td>
            <td>The mirror of <code>Min</code>: no ceiling at all. Alone it caps nothing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Selection — <code>if</code> / <code>else if</code> / <code>else</code></td>
            <td>A statement, not a method. It returns nothing.</td>
            <td>Runs its block only when the condition is true, and can do more than one thing: change the value <em>and</em> print a message, or refuse the heal outright.</td>
            <td>More lines than a one-line expression, and it puts the rule at the <em>call site</em> rather than inside the method that owns the value — so the next healing source has to repeat it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField] private int</code></td>
            <td>A field declaration, not a call — the form you have used since 1.3. Class body, never inside a method. The name and the starting value are yours to choose.</td>
            <td>A named value that survives every frame and appears in the Inspector as the designer's tuning knob.</td>
            <td>A value is not a rule. Anyone with the Inspector can set maximum health below current health while the game runs, and nothing warns them — your cap has to be written so that stays survivable.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code></td>
            <td>Callback. Unity calls it; you never do.</td>
            <td>Fires on the first frame two shapes overlap. The parameter is the <em>other</em> object's collider.</td>
            <td>Triggers only. Make the potion solid and this callback never runs — the collision callback takes over instead.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Collider2D.CompareTag(string)</code></td>
            <td><code>bool CompareTag(string tag)</code></td>
            <td>Asks the engine whether the collider's GameObject carries that tag.</td>
            <td>Exact spelling, and the tag has to exist in the project first. It is asked of whichever object owns the collider, which is why the collider lives on the root.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Destroy(Object)</code></td>
            <td><code>void Destroy(Object obj)</code></td>
            <td>Removes the object at the end of the frame.</td>
            <td>It is final. If the potion should stay on the ground until the player needs it, this is the wrong tool.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Debug.Log(object)</code></td>
            <td><code>void Debug.Log(object message)</code></td>
            <td>Prints to the Console — the only feedback this game has until Unit 5.</td>
            <td>Text only, and it prints when <em>you</em> call it. A heal that is refused silently prints nothing unless you print it yourself.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Three facts a signature cannot carry.</strong> First, the two <code>Mathf</code>
        tools hand you back a value — nothing changes until you assign it to the field that
        holds health, and a call whose result you drop is a line that does nothing at all.
        Second, an <code>if</code> is the only option here that can <em>report</em> as well as
        bound: a method that returns a number has no way to say "refused". Third,
        <code>Mathf.Clamp</code> bounds both ends in a single call, which is why it is the tool
        people reach for, and why you should be able to name its cost before you pick it.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Current health is 2, maximum health is 5, and a potion restores 3. Walk each tool in the table: what does health become, and which of them can tell you that part of the heal was wasted?</li>
<li>A spike drives health down while a potion drives it up. Which tool covers both ends in one call, and which two leave an end open?</li>
<li>Only one option in that table can print a message as well as change the value. Why can a method that returns a number not do that?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: The Potion</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        This one is on you, and there is no code in this section on purpose. Every piece of it
        is something you have already built once: a prefab with a trigger collider is 2.1, a
        method that changes health is 2.2, and a tag check is both. What is new is the
        <em>decision</em> — and the specification below names the file, the field, the method
        and the question to answer. The reference table names the API. The rest is yours.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the prefab.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>In the Hierarchy, right-click → <strong>2D Object → Sprite</strong>. Name it <strong>Potion</strong>, then drag it into <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> so it becomes reusable. What is the difference between the object in the scene and the asset in the folder?</li>
            <li>Which sprite will you use, and what Sprite Renderer <strong>Color</strong> tint makes it obviously <em>not</em> a coin at a glance?</li>
            <li>Add a collider. Trigger or solid — and what is your reason? Which of the two things you built this unit does that make it most like?</li>
            <li>You are about to create a tag that does not exist in this project. Where does the tag list actually live, and does the <em>prefab</em> need the tag or does one instance in the scene? How do you confirm the spelling before you write it in a string, and what happens if you get it wrong?</li>
            <li>Nothing is placed in the world yet. With the prefab finished, drag at least two instances into the Overworld scene, somewhere you will walk — one to test against a damaged player, and one to test against a full-health player.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the rule.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>What field holds the maximum health? What type and starting value, and how is it different from the current-health field? Which of the two is the designer's tuning knob, and which one is game state?</li>
            <li>Where exactly does the ceiling get applied — in the potion before it calls the player, or <em>inside</em> the method that changes health? Which of those cannot be bypassed by a future caller?</li>
            <li>Which tool from the reference table did you choose, and what does your choice do that the other three cannot?</li>
            <li>If you chose the tool that bounds both ends, what is the <em>lower</em> bound — and what does bounding at 0 mean for the death check you wrote in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>?</li>
            <li>If you bounded only one end, name the check that stops a spike driving health below zero, and say which method it lives in.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the trigger.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which callback, and which tag check?</li>
            <li>Which method on the player do you call, and do you pass a positive or a negative number? Why does one method have to serve both the spike and the potion?</li>
            <li>Do you destroy the potion? Which object — the collider's GameObject, or your own?</li>
            <li>What should happen when a player already at full health touches a potion: consume it, or leave it on the ground? Both are defensible. Pick one, and name the check your choice requires and where that check belongs.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>The Healing Decision</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which tool did you choose, and what does it cost you? A rule with no stated cost is not yet a decision.</li>
<li>Where did you put the ceiling — and how would you find out it was in the wrong place, using only the potion and a spike?</li>
<li>Your potion and your spike now call the same method with different signs. What would you have to duplicate if you gave them separate heal and damage methods?</li>
</ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save, press Play, and verify. Drop your health on a spike, then walk into a potion and
        confirm it comes back up. Then walk into another potion at full health and confirm your
        rule holds. Then set maximum health in the Inspector while the game is running, and see
        what your rule does when the ceiling moves underneath the value.
    </p>
</div>


<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain how the potion is like the coin, like the spike, and unlike both</li>
        <li>Create a prefab and a brand-new project tag from scratch, and place instances in the scene</li>
        <li>Explain the overheal exploit, and which rule prevents it</li>
        <li>Compare <code>if</code>, <code>Mathf.Min</code>, <code>Mathf.Max</code> and <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>, and defend the one you chose — including what it costs</li>
        <li>Explain why the rule lives in the method that changes health rather than in the potion</li>
        <li>Test at the boundary — health exactly at maximum — instead of only in the middle</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — selection, pickups and health in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the reference for trigger pickups</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Mathf and selection syntax</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I…? index</a> — find this lesson again by the question you were asking</li>
    </ul>
